    id: "bio-2016-ol-q10-c-i", topicId: "bio-1-5", conceptId: "fossil",
    section: "C", questionRef: "2016 OL Q10(c)(i)",
    stem: "The giant deer or ‘Irish Elk’ was a mammal that became extinct in Ireland about 9,000 years ago. Fossils of the ‘Irish Elk’ found in bogs and mud-dried lakes have been used to study the animal. Fossils are the remains of something that lived a long time ago and provide evidence that evolution has occurred. The study of these fossils has shown that the antlers of the deer measured about 3 m from tip to tip. Many possible causes, such as disease, severe cold or over-hunting have been put forward as to why the animal became extinct.",
