    id: "bio-2016-ol-q11-b-i", topicId: "bio-3-1", conceptId: "bee-hibernation",
    section: "C", questionRef: "2016 OL Q11(b)(i)",
    stem: "There are many bee species in Ireland. Some of these species are bumblebees. The queen bee comes out of hibernation in the spring and makes a nest, often in an old mouse hole. Fertilised eggs become female worker bees. Unfertilised eggs become males and these leave the colony. Bumblebees are very important for pollination. When the female bee lands on a flower, she collects nectar and pollen from the flower. Bee numbers are in decline, in part due to the loss of their habitats. Many bee habitats have been lost due to farming, forestry and housing. Conservation programmes will have to be introduced if we are to halt this decline.",
